course: dictionary
lesson: 8399
title: One word, several meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Many words have more than one meaning. The best way to find the right one is to read the words around it. «Your dog is fast asleep on the rug, but its paws are paddling.» A dog sleeps on a floor covering, so here a rug is a thick piece of cloth on the floor.
explain: Now look at this one: «Some horses need rugs in winter, especially clipped or thin-coated ones.» A horse does not lie on a floor covering in winter. The clue is the cold weather and the horse, so a rug here is a warm blanket.
explain: Some phrases have a meaning of their own. «Pull the rug out from under someone» is not about cloth on a floor. It means suddenly taking away their help. When a phrase seems odd, ask what the whole phrase means, not just each word.
fact: A rug is a thick piece of cloth that covers part of a floor.
fact: In the UK, a rug can also be a thick blanket, such as a travel rug for your knees on a train or a picnic.
fact: «Pull the rug out from under someone» means to suddenly take away their help.
fact: A word with more than one meaning is called a multiple-meaning word, and the context tells you which meaning is meant.
fact: «Snug as a bug in a rug» is a saying about feeling warm, safe and cosy.
exercise: «Your dog is fast asleep on the rug, and its paws are paddling.» What is a rug here? | A bowl for water | *A thick piece of cloth that covers part of a floor | A picnic blanket for your knees | A flying carpet in a story
exercise: «Some horses need rugs in winter.» In this sentence, what is a rug? | A rug on a classroom floor | A dog's bed | *A warm blanket for a horse | A grey, wet day
exercise: Mia says, «The team pulled the rug out from under us.» What does it mean? | They pulled a cloth off the floor | They won the game easily | They lay down on a rug to rest | *They suddenly took away the help they had promised
test: «She found a marble on the rug where her baby brother crawls.» What is the rug in this sentence? | *A thick cloth that covers the floor | A small bowl for water | A saying about feeling cosy | A warm blanket for a horse
test: Kai says a travel rug is a blanket. Which sentence uses rug in the same way? | The rug in the hall is blue and green | I bought a new rug for my bedroom floor | The rug was pulled out from under the table | *Dad wrapped a warm rug around my knees on the cold train
exam: What does «pull the rug out from under someone» mean? | To pull a floor covering away from someone | *To suddenly take away someone's help | To hug someone warmly | To tidy up a room
